\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 S}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13780479 earch }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 T}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13780479 ree
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 \tab 
\par \tab A Binary tree that has the property that all data elements in the }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 left sub-tree}{\insrsid14091316  of node n are }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 less}{\i\insrsid14091316  }{\insrsid14091316 
than contents of node }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 n}{\insrsid14091316  and elements in the }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 right sub-tree}{\i\insrsid14091316  }{\insrsid14091316 of node n are }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 greater
 than}{\insrsid14091316  or }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 equal}{\insrsid14091316  to the contents of node }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 n}{\insrsid14091316 , is termed as }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 Binary Search Tree.}{
\insrsid14091316\charrsid13071743 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\fs36\ul\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\insrsid14091316 There are 2 basic operations they are.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\insrsid14091316 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li960\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx960{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb1\pnstart1\pnindent360 {\pntxta .}}\faauto\ls1\rin0\lin960\itap0\pararsid14091316 {
\insrsid14091316 Insertion of the node in the Binary Search Tree
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\insrsid14091316 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li960\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx960{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent360 {\pntxta .}}\faauto\ls2\rin0\lin960\itap0\pararsid14091316 {
\insrsid14091316 Deletion of the node from the Binary Search Tree}{\fs28\insrsid14091316 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\b\i\fs28\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid7371392 Insertion of the node in the Binary tree}{\b\fs28\insrsid14091316\charrsid941647 : }{\b\fs28\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid941647 
\par }{\insrsid14091316  }{\fs28\insrsid14091316   
\par }{\insrsid14091316        The first part of our project is to insert the node in the tree. We can do by starting
\par With an empty binary tree and inserting one node at time at a time, always making
\par Sure that the properties of a search are preserved. The first case, inserting a node into 
\par a empty tree is easy. we need only make root point to the new node. If the tree is not empty, then we must compare the key with the one in the root. If it is less than the new node must be inserted 
into the left sub tree, if it is more then it must be inserted into 
\par the right sub tree. If the key value is equal, then assumption that no two nodes have 
\par the same key is violated.   
\par }{\b\ul\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\b\i\fs28\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid7371392 Deletion of the node from the binary tree}{\b\fs28\insrsid14091316\charrsid941647 :}{\b\fs28\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid941647 
\par }{\insrsid14091316 
\par       If a node to be deleted is a leaf then the process is easy: We need only replace the
\par  link of the deleting node by NULL. The process remains simple is the tree has only 
\par one sub tree: We just adjust the link the parent of the deletion node to its subtree.
\par      When the item to be deleted has both left and right sub trees nonempty. How ever
\par  the problem is more complicated. To which of the subtree should the parent of the deleted node. What we do is to attach the right subtree in place of the deleted node 
\par and then hang the left subtree onto appropriate node of the right subtree.
\par        To which node of the right subtree should the former left subtree be attached? 
\par since every key in left subtree precede every key of the right subtree, it must as far to 
\par the left as possible and this point can be found by taking left branches until left subtree
\par  is found.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 A}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13780479 lgorithm of}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316  }{
\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 B}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 ubble }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 S}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 ort
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 One of the most widely used sorting techniques is Bubble sort algorithm. It is easy to understand and to implement the program.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls24\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
The basic idea of Bubble sort is to pass through the file of records or an array of elements, sequentially many times until an exchange counter becomes 0 (or FALSE).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}During each such sequential pass, each element or record in the file is being compared with its successor. For instance (ary [k] > ary [k+1].
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Two such adjacent elements are interchanged immediately upon discovering that they are not in order say either Ascending or Descending.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}To reflect such interchange, exchange counter/variable is set to a 1(or TRUE).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}With this approach, at the most (n-1) passes or sequential scans are applied over an input array of n elements. (or a file of n records).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\insrsid14091316 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx2160\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls24\ilvl1\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
However, looping i.e., repeat for next pass need not be continued upto (n-1) passes, once exchange counter is set to a 0 (or FALSE) during any subsequent pass.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\insrsid14091316 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}
When exchange counter result to a 0 (or FALSE) at any intermediate pass, is an indication that all of the (including remaining ones) array elements or records are in proper order (i.e., Ascending or Descending).
\par }\pard \ql \fi360\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316     So exchange counter introduce
s a sort of check to determine whether any interchanges were made during that particular pass. It also helps for early closure of sorting process and thus saving on total sorting time.
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 If no interchanges have occurred means array elements or file of records are perfectly in order!
\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid10880682 
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316       Bubble Sort technique makes the smaller elements or records with smaller keys to move upwards or }{\i\insrsid14091316 bubble up }{\insrsid14091316 
in each subsequent pass (loop).
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316       Thus it is important to note that the largest element of the array will be positioned at n}{\super\insrsid14091316 th}{\insrsid14091316 
 location in the first pass. Looping for a second pass, will bring second largest element at (n-1) }{\super\insrsid14091316 th}{\insrsid14091316  location in the array.
\par       In other words, on each successive pass, the records with next largest key values will be placed in positions (n-1) (n-2) (n-3)\'852, respectively. Thus resulting into a sorted table of records.
\par 
\par  }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid5074526 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid12597650 BUBBLE_SORT (A)}{\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid12597650 1. pass <- 1}{\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid12597650 2. sorted <- false
\par 3. while (not sorted) and (pass < n)
\par 4.    do sorted <- true
\par 5.       for i <- 0 to n-pass-1
\par 6.          do if A[i] > A[i+1]
\par 7.                then exchange A[i] <-> A[i+1]
\par 8.                     sorted <- false
\par 9.       pass <- pass + 1
\par }\pard \qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 A}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13780479 lgorithm of}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316  }{
\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 S}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 election }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 S}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 ort
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\f36\ul\insrsid14091316 
\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 \tab 
When sorting is done by selecting appropriate elements in order and placing them into their respective sorted positions, then such method is termed as Selection Sort.
\par \tab Selection Sort technique can be developed as a general algorithm that make use of a }{\i\insrsid14091316 descending priority queue.
\par }{\insrsid14091316 \tab Elements of the input array to be sorted are preprocessed by inserting them one by one into a priority queue. To arrange them in successively by selection, a function that returns largest element of a priority queue can be used. Su
ch an algorithm is called the general }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid9778339 selection sort technique}{\insrsid14091316   }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid8877849 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316\charrsid15939754 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid11687102 SELECTION_SORT (A)
\par 1. for i <- 0 to n-2
\par 2.    do current <- i
\par 3.       for k <- i+1 to n-1 do
\par 4.          do if A[current] > A[k]
\par 5.                then current <- k
\par 6.       exchange A[i] <-> A[current]
\par }\pard \qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 A}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13780479 lgorithm of}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316  }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 I}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 nsertion }{
\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 S}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 ort
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316           
\par             Insertion sort technique works very well when n, the size of the array is small and a few input elements are already in sorted order. Simple Insertion sor
t technique sorts elements from an unsorted array by inserting them into a sorted array in such a way that the resulting array is also sorted.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls20\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
To start with, in this method, we assume that the first element of a given input array is in its proper position (i.e., sorted position)
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Now the elements from unsorted array may precede or follow this sorted element once after performing necessary compar
ison with the sorted one, in order to keep them in their proper position for sorted order like Ascending.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}The process of repeated comparison and insertion continues until all elements of the unsorted array are placed into their sorted position.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 The array is scanned from }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid11489816 left}{\insrsid14091316  to }{\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid11489816 right}{\i\insrsid14091316 .}{
\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par }{\insrsid14091316 INSERTION_SORT (A)
\par 1. for k <- 1 to n-1
\par 2.    do x <- A[k]
\par 3.       i <- k - 1
\par 4.       found <- false
\par 5.       while (not found) and (i >= 0)
\par 6.          do if A[i] > x
\par 7.                then exchange A[i+1] <-> A[i]
\par 8.                     i <- i - 1
\par 9.                else found <- true
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 A}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13780479 lgorithm of}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316  }{
\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 M}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 erge }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 S}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 ort
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\f36\insrsid14091316 
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 \tab When operation of merging is applied to a single unsorted array, the resulting procedure is termed as }{
\i\insrsid14091316\charrsid9778339 Merge Sort}{\i\insrsid14091316 .
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls21\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
Merge sort procedure involves splitting of the given unsorted single file of records into tow parts.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}To start with, given single unsorted file or array is to be treated as set of n files or n array each with single record or element.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Now merge adjacent pairs of files or array to contain 
records or elements in sorted order. This kind of merge sorting of neighboring records/ elements yields approximately n/2 files of size 2 records/elements each, during first pass.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Repeat the merge sorting process to merge each neighboring pairs of files until the resultant files or arrays reduces to a single sorted file or sorted array of size n.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par    }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid9778339 
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316\charrsid4487296 MERGESORT (A, lo, hi)
\par  1. if lo < hi then
\par  }{\lang3082\langfe1033\langnp3082\insrsid14091316\charrsid15669710 2.    mi}{\lang3082\langfe1033\langnp3082\insrsid14091316\charrsid4487296 d = (lo + hi) / 2";
\par  3.    MERGESORT (A, lo, mid)
\par  }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid4487296 4.    MERGESORT (A, mid+1, hi)
\par  5.    L = lo
\par  6.    H = mid + 1
\par  7.    for k <- lo to hi
\par  8.       do if L <= mid and (H > hi or A[L] < A[H])
\par  9.             then scratch[k] = A[L]
\par 10.                  L <- L + 1
\par 11.             else scratch[k] = A[H]
\par 12.                  H <- H + 1
\par 13.    for k <- lo to hi}{\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid4487296 14.       do A[k] <- scratch[k]}{\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 A}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13780479 lgorithm of}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316  }{
\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 Q}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 uick }{\b\f36\fs52\ul\insrsid14091316\charrsid13581555 S}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid14091316 ort
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440\faauto\ls25\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 Quicksort 
technique works very well on large sets of records. It has a very good average behavior among all the sorting techniques. This sorting technique is developed by C.A.R.Hoare
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Quicksort technique essentially partions the table of records or an array of elements into two sub-tables or sub-arrays. Therefore the name Partition Exchange sort.
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316                 
\par    }{\b\insrsid14091316 How it partition?
\par }{\b\insrsid14091316\charrsid3491925 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\faauto\ls26\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
At each step of this method, the main objective is to place or fix a particular record (or an array element) at its final position within the table (array).
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 If Ascending order is required, then the partition that precedes this poitioned or fixed, record, c
ontains smaller elements (i.e., all are greater then key elements/positioned elements).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid14091316 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\faauto\ls26\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 While fixing a particular element\rquote 
s final position, the element is compared with all elements once starting from left (array index 0) and next from right (array index 0). When the two elements are not in order, their position is
 marked, elements get interchanged and process of comparison will be repeated for subsequent elements in the array. At last, the intended element will be placed finally at its unique position. T
his procedure will be applied reclusively on individual partitions to fix positions to fix position of each element. 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14091316 {\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086 QUICKSORT (A, p, r)
\par }{\insrsid14091316  }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086 1. if p < r
\par }{\insrsid14091316  }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086 2. then q <- PARTITION (A, p, r)
\par }{\insrsid14091316  }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086 3.}{\insrsid14091316  }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086 QUICKSORT (A, p, q)
\par }{\insrsid14091316  }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086 4. QUICKSORT (A, q+1, r)
\par }{\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086 PARTITION (A, p, r)
\par  1. piv <- A[p]
\par  2. i <- p - 1
\par  3. j <- r + 1}{\insrsid14091316 
\par }{\insrsid14091316\charrsid1972086  4. while TRUE
\par  5. do repeat j <- j - 1
\par  6. until A[j] <= piv
\par  7. repeat i <- i + 1
\par  8. until A[i] >= piv
\par  9. if i < j
\par 10. then exchange A[i] <-> A[j]
\par 11. else return j}{\insrsid14091316 
\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid13780479 {\b\fs28\insrsid13780479 
\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid8343633 {\b\f36\fs48\insrsid8343633\charrsid8343633        }{\b\f36\fs48\insrsid14091316 
\par 
\par        }{\b\f36\fs48\insrsid8343633\charrsid8343633  }{\b\f36\fs48\ul\insrsid13780479\charrsid8343633 F}{\b\f36\fs28\ul\insrsid13780479\charrsid8343633 UTURE }{\b\f36\fs48\ul\insrsid13780479\charrsid8343633 S}{
\b\f36\fs28\ul\insrsid13780479\charrsid8343633 COPE}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid13780479\charrsid13780479 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid13780479 {\b\fs28\ul\insrsid13780479\charrsid9113065 
\par }{\insrsid13780479       
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid13780479 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\faauto\ls18\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid13780479 {\insrsid13780479 
The project can be extended to include all the shortcomings listed in previous section.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid13780479 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}The project only animates the }{\insrsid76608 numbers, it}{\insrsid13780479  can include having characters to be shorted.
\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid13780479 {\insrsid13780479 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid10559920 {\b\fs28\insrsid7619103 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid7619103 {\b\f36\fs48\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 L}{\b\f36\fs28\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 IMITATIONS}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13780479  }{
\b\f36\fs48\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 O}{\b\f36\fs28\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 F}{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13780479  }{\b\f36\fs48\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 T}{\b\f36\fs28\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 HE}
{\b\f36\fs40\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13780479  }{\b\f36\fs48\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 P}{\b\f36\fs28\ul\insrsid10559920\charrsid13581555 ROJECT}{\f36\fs40\insrsid10559920\charrsid13780479 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid10559920 {\insrsid10559920 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\insrsid10559920 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\faauto\ls17\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid10559920 {\insrsid10559920 O}{\insrsid941647 ur project only accepts}{
\insrsid10559920  number.
